From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: VirtSem: VirtBiz

Let the games begin!  This can indeed be a round-table seminar, as many 
Extropian folks are involved in projects along the lines of doing
business in virtual worlds:

* Doug Barnes works at io.com, and Geoff Dale has set up an 
Extropian Institute site on their MOO.
* Jamie's involved in a company defining network commerce 
models, although he's under obligation to not divulge many
specifics of this company.
* Derek Zahn's multimedia mail program.
* My "Net Storefront" strategy, which involves a client software toolkit
and simple high-level protocols over MIME (a multimedia mail format
which will hopefully become standard) for distributing virtual store 
windows, catalogs, contracts, invoices, receipts, and other forms,
and information products ranging from text and software to audio and 
video, in a secure fashion.
* Dean Tribble's Memex, commercializing concepts created by AMIX
and Xanadu.
* Tim May's visions of "cypherspace", Habitat 2, etc. 

All of these are fascinating, and I'd love to hear more about them!
(And if I failed to mention your project, my apologies, feel free to
speak up and let us know what you're doing).

I posit the following: real-time interaction is not as important 
in virtual worlds.  When people are in virtual worlds they usually
like to dispense with real-time constraints and deal with the world as
if it were a "to do" list.  This is why, for example, e-mail is so much 
more popular and effective than "talk".  My ideal virtual world 
would be 3D, as in "True Names", _Neuromancer_, etc., but the most common
temporal structures would be queues: e-mail for interaction with people, 
undoable interactions with the virtual environment, etc.  Real-time 
interaction would be a special case, something like mail tagged "urgent" 
only moreso.

