From: cman@caffeine.io.com (Douglas Barnes)
Subject: VirtSem: VirtBiz

I do like Nick's ideas about structuring things like catalogs, 
presenting products, and handling the transactions using a MIME-
like format that is (one hopes) standard across a variety of servers.
However, I must certainly beg to differ with him when he deprecates
the need for interactivity.

First, some background. Although I'm not actively involved in current 
development of our MOO (metaverse.io.com 7777), I'm technically the 
founder and ArchWizard there. Since I'm a Unix geek before I'm a MOO
geek, my talents have been needed elsewhere and I've had to make myself
scarce there because it's quite addictive.

We belong to a gregarious species, and baseline members enjoy each
others' company a great deal, even when abstracted through a low-
bandwidth text-only interface. Despite their low bandwidth, MOOs, 
MUDs and their ilk are so compelling to some people that they forsake 
all else, including grades, loved ones, jobs and long-practiced hobbies. 

Clearly some powerful force is at work here, and I think we can place
most of it at the feet of the joys of interaction. 

Now how does this impact commerce? As good extropians, it would be a
(a what, a sin? do extropians have sins?) to forgo a chance to make a
buck. Where does most non-food retail commerce happen? Shopping malls.

Do people go to a shopping mall with a checklist that they want to
methodically check off? Not bloody likely. Nick might, and I might, but
go look at what most of the other members of your species are doing at
the mall sometime. They sure as hell aren't spending most, or even a 
really large percentage, of their time buying or engaged in goal-seeking
shopping. They do buy stuff; in fact, they buy the vast majority of 
their stuff there. But this is almost entirely incidental to real 
motivations for going to the mall. 

Sure there's going to be a market for services that let you quickly and
efficiently home in on the nine widgets you want to buy optimized for
price and reputation of the vendor. But folks will also continue to try
and buy when such opportunities are presented to them while they're
picking lice off each other, engaging in mating behavior, looking
for a sunny spot on a rock, or...  hanging out with their virtual pals
in a MOO.

Certainly Nick's ideas have great merit _as an adjunct_ to providing
an interactive experience. Goodness knows vendors want to be able to
hand someone a nicely produced brochure, catalog or whatever. But these
are really just props, tools that help an effective interactive 
salesman close a deal. What I want to be able to do is interactively
hand someone an object in Nick's format in my interactive environment,
and have it automagically transmitted to the recipient, let them read
it, and when they come back, do my closing pitch -- interactively.

Nick also seems stuck on the notion that MOOs are inherently squeezed
into vt100 format. On one hand, they do reap all of the benefits of
allowing an even lower common denominator than vt100; line at a time
telnet. However, there are MOOs that exist right now that support HTML,
and we are involved in a variety of research into other client-oriented 
approaches.

Of course, things are fairly crude right now. We're still exploring
the idea, playing around with it. But it is the height of folly to
think that purchases and sales will happen in a vacuum of interactivity.

