From: Eli Brandt <ebrandt@jarthur.Claremont.EDU>
Subject: Random Thoughts

> From: onomoto@netcom.com (Young and Loud)
>         Actually, something I'd consider really nifty would be a retina
> tracker in a virtal worlds setting -- since the system knows what you're
> looking at pt A, it could render with maximal detail at A, and decrease
> detail (sample greater areas) Gaussian-like (or something like that) as it
> gets further away -- radial-wise.  

I saw a demo of something like this.  The user sat in one place and
didn't move their head much, while an IR-reflective eye tracker
noted where they were looking and blockified the rest of the screen
accordingly.  It looked quite strange to everyone else.  I'm not
sure how much this actually buys you if you have z-buffer graphics
hardware -- I guess the system can fudge the texture mapping where
you aren't looking.  And it's great for directing super-/sub-sampling
for real-time raytracing.  ;-)

The pupil-dilation idea is interesting but might be hard to use.
There'd be gross noise from variations in light level, which would
be hard to correct for.  I'm sure virtual marketers would *love*
to have this kind of data, though.  They'd probably pay you to
window-shop.

I've been thinking that you could get a lot out of an EEG input
device even if it could only register a bit or two.  This is how
many buttons a lot of mice have, after all.  Combined with an
eye-tracker, this would be the ultimate input device for certain
purposes: selection, marking, moving.  Look at a file, `click', look
at a directory, `click', and you've moved a file -- in about 100
milliseconds.  You could do this now, except that few-bit EEG input
is too slow and uncertain, and eye-trackers may not have the angular
resolution.

