> like to dispense with real-time constraints and deal with the world as
> if it were a "to do" list.  This is why, for example, e-mail is so much 
> more popular and effective than "talk".

In general, I don't think this is true.  There are real-time virtual
worlds, such as MU*s, and off-line ones, such as certain
"storytelling" newsgroups.  The real-time ones are far more
popular.  It's true that talk isn't too popular, but irc is.  And
irc is closer to a virtual world than is the phone-like "talk".
"Effectiveness", of course, is a different matter.

Now, in the context of business, you may be right.  When ordering
mail-order, I'd much rather fire off an e-mail order than call and
be put on hold, eventually talking to someone who's just going to
enter my order into a computer.  This is a waste of real-time, and
real-time costs money.  On the other hand, cellulose bookstores
would lose a good bit of my business if I couldn't walk in and do
some real-time browsing and impulse buying.

There is a substantial niche for non-real-time business, and it may
even turn out to be an Internet success story.  But there is also
going to be strong demand for real-time interaction; schmoozing,
browsing, and recreation, as opposed to placing a pre-defined order.

